FPS at 640 still 6 → not fill-rate; likely CPU-bound in JS or draw call overhead in SwiftShader. 822 draw calls ~ maybe heavy in software. Real GPU 822 calls is a lot but could run 30fps mid-range. We should reduce draw calls to improve both testability and mobile performance.

Biggest contributors: containers (113 × ~4 meshes + glint = ~565) and buildings (~30 × 15 = 450), zombies (134 × 6+ = 800 but culled to nearby). Total render calls 822.

Optimize:
1. Containers: replace crate group (body+lid+2 straps = 4 meshes) with a single mesh (box) + glint. Duffel: bag+strap=2 → 1. Barrel: 1. That saves ~300 draw calls. Visual loss minor. Use a merged simple crate geometry: BoxGeometry with scale; lid as separate? skip. Could use one box with material color and an emissive small lock. Keep glint.
2. Zombies: merge body/head/arms/legs into a single geometry? We can keep meshes but visibility culling: currently each zombie mesh is visible if d<170, all 134 within? At spawn, zombies spread across map; those >170 hidden → maybe 20 visible near. Render calls 822 includes culled. So not the main.
3. Buildings: merge meshes per building would help. Could use THREE.BufferGeometryUtils not available, but write a utility to merge geometries by material. Many materials shared (M.wood etc.), so we can batch all static boxes in the world by material into a handful of merged meshes. This is the biggest win. Approach:
   - Instead of adding wall/window/clutter boxes as separate meshes, collect them into arrays by material and merge at the end. But buildings also have doors (dynamic), roofs, etc. Colliders remain.
   - Implement batching wrapper: `box(parent,...)` currently adds mesh immediately. Changing everything is invasive.

   Alternative: after building the world, traverse sceneRoot, collect static Mesh objects that are direct children (not doors, glints), group by material, merge their box geometries via manual transform, create one merged mesh per material, remove originals. This is feasible:
     - For each mesh with geometry.type === 'BoxGeometry' (most world meshes), world matrix apply to a cloned position-normal-uv attribute; collect by material.
     - Skip meshes whose parent isn't sceneRoot? All boxes are added to sceneRoot directly or groups (cars, tents). Doors need dynamic rotation: their panels are children of pivot groups. If we merge those, doors won't animate. Skip meshes with any ancestor group marked dynamic (doors, structure). For static props, fine.
     - Merge per material with a simple function building arrays. BoxGeometry is non-indexed? THREE.BoxGeometry is indexed with 24 verts, 36 indices. Handle index.
     - After merging, remove original meshes. This could reduce static boxes from ~600 to ~15 draw calls. Huge.
     - Cars/tents/towers/windows/roofs all merged.
     - Instanced meshes untouched.
     - Interactables rely on data not meshes, fine. Container meshes need highlight/visibility toggling: containers glint separate; crate group meshes would be merged if we merge groups → breaking search visuals (they should disappear? currently crates stay, only glint hides; so merging crates is fine except container.mat highlight unused). We don't highlight crates currently, so safe. Container.mesh used only for drop/hide? We set c.mesh but never hide on searched except glint. Fine.
     - Door pivots: pivots are Groups added to sceneRoot; merging children would break animations. Skip any mesh with ancestor group having `userData.dynamic` or specifically door.pivot. Mark pivot groups with `userData.dynamic=true`. Or skip groups: only merge meshes that are direct children of sceneRoot OR children of static groups? Car groups can be merged. Simpler: merge only meshes with parent === sceneRoot (most boxes) and leave car/tent groups. That reduces many.
       - Walls, roofs, windows, clutter, porch, barrels, silos (meshes in groups), hmm silo group children not merged.
       - Buildings: walls/roofs/windows/porch boxes are direct sceneRoot children via box(sceneRoot,...). ~12 per building ×30 = 360.
       - Town dressing lamps/benches direct or group.
     - Also doors: lintel direct, pivot group skip.
   - Implement mergeStaticMeshes() in world.js after layout/build; group by material uuid and geometry type BoxGeometry. Use mesh.matrixWorld. Since sceneRoot at origin no transform.
   - Need normals transform (normal matrix) and uv. BoxGeometry has uv. Some meshes have rotation (roof slabs, benches groups not direct).
   - Materials include textured? no, all colors; merges by material instance. Many pick() materials reference shared M materials, good. But addBuilding picks random materials per building from shared set → few materials. Windows glass shared. Roof materials shared. Great: merged boxes → maybe 10 draw calls.
   - For mesh scale/rotation: apply matrixWorld. For normal: transform direction via normalMatrix.
   - Some meshes use cone/cylinder (roof gable, silos) skip for now.
   - Merge only direct sceneRoot mesh children (isMesh && geometry BoxGeometry && parent===sceneRoot && !mesh.userData.dynamic).
   - Add merged meshes to sceneRoot; remove originals.
   - Draw calls drop by ~350 → ~470. Containers optimize to single mesh → ~400. Still high because zombies/cars/fences? Fences: addFenceRow adds direct sceneRoot posts and rails (many) → merged too! Great ~150 saved.
   - This should get calls to maybe 250 and FPS up.

Also reduce shadows none.

4. Reduce building clutter containers (113 crates ×4) — single mesh crate saves ~340 calls. Combined → maybe 120 calls. Excellent.

Implement:
- In world.js add `mergeStaticBoxes()` called at end of buildWorld.
- Change buildContainers to single-mesh crates (body only; add lid as part of same? use one box plus a thin dark stripe? One mesh). Keep glint.
- Also remove car group meshes? Not merged (parent groups), ~6 meshes each ×5 cars = 30. Fine.
- Zombies: 6 meshes per zombie visible; if 30 visible → 180 calls. Merge each zombie type into a single geometry? Animate limbs needs separate; could merge torso+head into one and legs/arms into 4 → 5. Minor. Leave.

